Touch input on Windows: taps click, drags scroll, long presses right-click.

gpui 0.2.2 never looks at WM_POINTER, so touch falls through to Windows'
legacy handling. That promotes a tap to a mouse click but turns a drag into
a pan gesture that only scrolls windows with native scrollbars, so on a
touchscreen nothing in rox scrolled. The backend now consumes touch
pointers itself and feeds them to a recognizer that speaks the events gpui
already dispatches:

- A tap is a hover move plus a left click, with its own double-tap window
  (400ms, 16px) since the system's double-click box is sized for a mouse.
- A drag past 8px scrolls the container under where it started, locked to
  its dominant axis, and a fast release coasts on UIScrollView's decay.
  Touching a fling stops it.
- Holding still for 500ms and lifting is a right click, which opens context
  menus. Holding and then moving is a left-button drag from the original
  point, which is how sliders, splitters and drag-and-drop work by touch.

Pen and touchpad pointers keep the default handling. One contact is
followed at a time and pinch isn't recognized. Upstream gpui has since
grown a portable TouchGestureRecognizer behind its own touch events, with
the Windows side still in review (zed #64205); on a bump that carries both,
this patch goes and rox moves to their long-press and touch-drag APIs.

